# Token definitions for `contract.vos`, the authored text of a VOS contract
# package outside Warrant (VOS02 sections 2.5 and 6.8, decision D-GD6).
# @version 1
#
# A contract file is one record per line:
#
#   vos-contract 1
#   name vos-sched-contract
#   iface sched
#   tier any
#   config N 8
#   ceiling dispatch steps 160
#   end
#
# Three kinds of token are enough.  The words of the file ("name", "tier",
# "ceiling", "depth", ...) are ordinary NAME tokens; the parser grammar names
# them by value.  Which CLASS of name a NAME must be (a hyphen-only package
# name, an underscore-only operation name, a letters-digits-underscore key,
# at most 32 bytes) depends on the row it sits in, which one lexical pattern
# cannot know, so `vos-manifest` checks the class of each value after the
# parse.  The same goes for the numeric range of a DEC (see below).
#
# Whitespace.  A single space between the words of a row is skipped.  A tab, a
# carriage return and every other byte outside the patterns below is a lexical
# error (the lexer reports "Unexpected sequence"), so the file is ASCII text
# with LF line ends by construction.  Doubled spaces, a trailing space and extra
# line feeds after the `end` row are accepted by this grammar; the canonical-form
# check (parse, re-emit, compare byte for byte) rejects them.  That is the
# section 6.1 discipline: ONE spelling per object.

skip:
  SPACE = /[ ]+/

# A line end.  The record structure is line-based, so it is a real token.
NEWLINE = /\n/

# A non-negative decimal number: `0`, or a digit run with no leading zero.  At
# most 20 digits (the digit count of u64::MAX).  A longer run lexes as a DEC
# followed by another DEC, which the parser grammar refuses.  That the value
# also fits u64 is a numeric check the grammar cannot make (vos-manifest).
DEC = /0|[1-9][0-9]{0,19}/

# Every word and every name.  The first character is a letter, so a NAME never
# starts like a number.
NAME = /[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*/
